=== Introduction
--- Psalms 33–35 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalms 33–35
sub: Sing unto him a new song • Blessed is the nation whose God is the LORD • Plead my cause, O LORD
refs: Psalm 33:3; Psalm 33:12; Psalm 35:1
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 33
--- Psalm 33 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 33
sub: A new song • The counsel of the LORD standeth for ever
refs: Psalm 33:1

--- Praise the Lord with harp
kicker: Psalm 33 · A new song
scripture: Psalm 33:1-3 | mark: Praise the LORD with harp; Sing unto him a new song; play skilfully with a loud noise
p: **Psalm 33** opens with the clearest enforcement of writing, singing and performing songs of praise and worship to Almighty God. On stringed instruments, especially. Performing skillfully and with a loud noise within the congregation, songs with the doctrine of scripture, with the truth of His mighty works being honored.
p: God's righteousness and judgment, His power and creation, the fear and adoration He commands from those that love the Lord to sing unto Him.

--- Psalms and hymns and spiritual songs
kicker: Application • Music
p: Book of Psalms explicitly names several musical instruments, including the harp, psaltery, timbrel, cymbal, trumpet, cornet, organ, and pipe. These are given instructions by the Psalmist and the music leader, and are used to provide melody and concordance to the congregational singing. An activity that we are encouraged to perform in the New Testament church.
big: Colossians 3:16 | mark: in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs
p: Music is a form of learning and worship, as much as prayer, and it is edifying to the soul when focused on this spiritual truth, that it is for His sake that we make melodies.
big: Ephesians 5:19 | mark: making melody in your heart to the Lord

--- Decently and in order
kicker: Application • Music
p: What it is not, is an excuse to be carnal, to satisfy the flesh with worldly beats and discordant instruments, mimicking a rock concert or depraved discotheque.
p: Congregational singing stirs the soul, lifting your voice up to the Lord. But many worship services turn into a tantric trance, lifting up hands and swaying, captivated by the music and beats, lasting hours at some churches. With songs that are thinly veiled pop love songs, with little to no doctrine applied.
p: Other churches take the instruments completely out of the equation, which seems at odds with the details given in the Bible.
p: There is a middle ground, and a place for various musical accompaniments, while maintaining the orderly and decent service that all things at church should be done in.

--- A new song in heaven
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 33:3
p: One thing that is clear, Heaven is a place full of song and worship.
scripture: Revelation 5:9-10 | mark: they sung a new song
p: And
scripture: Revelation 14:2-3 | mark: the voice of harpers harping with their harps

--- The song of Moses and the Lamb
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 33:3
p: And again
scripture: Revelation 15:2-4 | mark: having the harps of God

--- What a day that will be!
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 33:3
p: You can see in their singing and playing, that it pleases God, and the words being sung give witness to the truth that He is great and His judgments holy, and it is right to give the Lord praise and blessings through music. In that great by and by, we will be there, singing His praise. What a day that will be!
quote: When we all get to heaven, / What a day of rejoicing that will be! / When we all see Jesus, / We’ll sing and shout the victory! | When We All Get to Heaven

--- The word of the Lord is right
kicker: Psalm 33 · Dripping with doctrine
p: Like all good spiritual songs, **Psalm 33** is dripping with doctrine, especially the inspired Psalms, because:
big: Psalm 33:4
p: His word and works are right and truth. Foundational doctrine. Amen.

--- The goodness of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 33 · Dripping with doctrine
big: Psalm 33:5 | mark: the earth is full of the goodness of the LORD
p: Again, I say, Amen!

--- By the word of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 33 · Creation
scripture: Psalm 33:6-7 | mark: By the word of the LORD were the heavens made
p: A song about singing the praises of the Lord that also teaches why. The creation of all things, by His Word, and brought into being by inspiration. The hosts of Heaven, the gatherings of the sea. His truth and righteous judgment.

--- He spake, and it was done
kicker: Psalm 33 · Stand in awe
scripture: Psalm 33:8-9 | mark: Let all the earth fear the LORD; For he spake, and it was done
p: Amen, amen, amen!
p: We should sing of His glory. Why? Because of His might, we should fear Him, Who spoke all things into being. Who commanded and it was done. Whose Word made the foundations stand fast. Amen!
p: When taken as an instrument of teaching, Psalms serve to give perfect understanding to those that are singing. Models of prayer and adoration, given as examples and encouragement to those that fear the Lord.

--- The counsel of the heathen
kicker: Psalm 33 · The counsel of the Lord
big: Psalm 33:10 | mark: The LORD bringeth the counsel of the heathen to nought
p: The pride and arrogance of man, who without God, or His wisdom, attempt to plan their greatness, only to be dashed against the Rock. Only to have it all come to nothing.
p: Contrast this futility with this truth;
big: Psalm 33:11 | mark: The counsel of the LORD standeth for ever
p: The knowledge of His ways, revealed by His word, through the oracles and prophets of God, bless a people.

--- Blessed is the nation
kicker: Psalm 33 · His own inheritance
big: Psalm 33:12 | mark: Blessed is the nation whose God is the LORD
p: Note this is inclusive, not exclusive to one nation. Those He hath chosen for His own inheritance, any people that trust in Him, any nation that makes Him their God, is blessed.
big: Galatians 3:29 | mark: then are ye Abraham’s seed

--- Joint-heirs with Christ
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 33:12
p: Because believers are elect in Christ, to receive these blessings as joint-heirs.
scripture: Romans 8:16-17 | mark: joint-heirs with Christ
p: This offer is extended to the whole world. At the time of David, the king understood that Israel was elected to be a beacon of God's light, not an elitist and special nation, to only know the true God.

--- A light to the Gentiles
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 33:12
p: As the Bible says in **Isaiah 49:6** *I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles, that thou mayest be my salvation unto the end of the earth.*
big: Isaiah 49:6 | mark: I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles
p: The only advantage Israel had was receiving the oracles of God, and the promise to David to bring about the Christ from his line, and stewardship of the city where He chose to place His name. Otherwise they'd have been as Sodom.

--- He fashioneth their hearts alike
kicker: Psalm 33 · All the sons of men
scripture: Psalm 33:13-15 | mark: He fashioneth their hearts alike
p: We are all the same in His eyes. We are all guilty before Him, convicted in sin. All are in need of a savior.
p: The modern form of Jewish ethnic supremacy, a concept that as God's chosen people, it is because of their own qualities and holiness, is wicked. God is not a respecter of persons.

--- An horse is a vain thing
kicker: Psalm 33 · No king saved by an host
scripture: Psalm 33:16-17 | mark: An horse is a vain thing for safety
p: He judges individuals by faith and nations by works. And the offer to give His blessings to any king who chose to place their trust in Him, rather than strength of horses and size of armies, was always extended.
p: Nineveh was spared by their faith, believing the reluctant prophet Jonah. King Nebuchadnezzar's humbling 7 years in the wilderness struck with madness, led to him proclaiming the truth of God to the world. The Queen of the South came to Solomon in order to understand the truth of God.

--- The eye of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 33 · Them that fear him
scripture: Psalm 33:18-19 | mark: the eye of the LORD is upon them that fear him
p: Whosoever will come, He shall deliver.
p: And so we sing.

--- Our soul waiteth for the Lord
kicker: Psalm 33 · Our help and our shield
scripture: Psalm 33:20-22 | mark: Our soul waiteth for the LORD
center: Amen!

=== Psalm 34
--- Psalm 34 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 34
sub: When David changed his behaviour before Abimelech. Psalm 34 is taught in The Psalms of David's Flight.
refs: Psalm 34:1
read: davids-flight-1-the-flight-begins | The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 1

=== Psalm 35
--- Psalm 35 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 35
sub: Plead my cause, O LORD
refs: Psalm 35:1

--- Plead my cause
kicker: Psalm 35 · Fight against them
scripture: Psalm 35:1-2 | mark: Plead my cause, O LORD
p: **Psalm 35** is a model of prayer that carries with it both the plea for deliverance, and imprecatory, or negative prayers against enemies who seek our hurt.
p: In David's case, we understand that he was falsely accused, had enemies trying to literally kill him and destroy him.
p: We may not have the threat against our lives, but we can relate.

--- Our advocate
kicker: Application • Prayer
p: The Lord is our Judge of judges. We go boldly before the throne of grace, asking anything, that we may plead our cause. Asking God to get involved in our lives, to fight our fights.
p: We have such an awesome advocate in Christ Jesus and a Father Whose mercy and judgement is righteous and true. It is foolishness not to go before Him in prayer, knowing that He seeks our prosperity. Not the riches and accumulations of things, as the false teachers preach, but our overall benefit and success spiritually, that we may do His will.
p: So, of course when we have enemies in this life that seek our hurt, it would be natural that we get God involved.

--- Draw out also the spear
kicker: Psalm 35 · I am thy salvation
big: Psalm 35:3 | mark: say unto my soul, I am thy salvation
p: *Draw out also the spear, and stop the way against them that persecute me*. That is to form a flank before us, a hedge of protection, like a wall of men, spear extended. A host to shield us from the advances against us. To show us and comfort us with the truth that He is our salvation.

--- The angel of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 35 · Let them be confounded
scripture: Psalm 35:4-6 | mark: let the angel of the LORD chase them
p: Let our enemies be confounded and confused. Let them be scattered as the chaff. Let the angel of the Lord be our guardian. His children have guardian angels;
big: Hebrews 1:14 | mark: ministering spirits
p: We can call upon Him to give them direction to not only shield us, but to persecute them that seek to do us harm.

--- Worship God
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 35:5–6
p: But we shouldn't misunderstand that these ministering spirits are ours to control, or to plead to them. Many pray directly to angels, or archangels, which is wicked.
scripture: Revelation 22:8-9 | mark: See thou do it not; worship God
p: We pray, as David did, to the Living God. He directs His hosts.

--- Worshipping of angels
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 35:5–6
p: The Bible says that false teachers will mislead people into worshiping angels.
big: Colossians 2:18 | mark: worshipping of angels
p: Pray directly to God in all things. Pray for Him to dispatch His angels to defend us and attack our enemies, as David demonstrates here.

--- Their net in a pit
kicker: Psalm 35 · Without cause
scripture: Psalm 35:7-8 | mark: let his net that he hath hid catch himself
p: Like David, our enemies may seek to trap us, secretly. Digging pits for us to fall into, snares to catch us, to spoil us, taking what is ours, even if it is simply our reputation, and in doing so, ruin our testimony to others.
p: If they seek to ruin us in secret, then let the Lord's destruction come upon them without warning. Let their plans come to nothing and better still, catch them up in their own devices.

--- Love thy enemies?
kicker: Psalm 35 · My soul shall be joyful
scripture: Psalm 35:9-10 | mark: And my soul shall be joyful in the LORD
p: But, what about *love thy enemies*?
p: Notice that David is pleading for their destruction, not only for their acts against him, but because of their false witness and betrayal.

--- Evil for good
kicker: Psalm 35 · False witnesses
scripture: Psalm 35:11-12 | mark: They rewarded me evil for good
p: They rewarded his good with evil.

--- When they were sick
kicker: Psalm 35 · As my friend or brother
scripture: Psalm 35:13-14 | mark: when they were sick, my clothing was sackcloth
p: When they were sick, he humbled himself in prayer and fasting. He sought intercession for them with the Lord. He shows them love.
p: This is what we are to do, what Jesus asked of us.
big: Matthew 5:44 | mark: Love your enemies

--- Not contradictory
kicker: Application • Love your enemies
p: This seems contradictory, but it's not. When they are weak, or in need, we are to love them. To pray for their healing and to give to them the charity we wish they'd show us.
p: We may win them as brothers, or heap hot coals on their heads by our kindness.
p: But we do not have any obligations to pray they succeed in the evil they do, and while we should not seek vengeance ourselves, we can leave it to the Judge of all to take up our cause and dispatch justice against them.

--- Hypocritical mockers in feasts
kicker: Psalm 35 · In mine adversity
scripture: Psalm 35:15-16 | mark: With hypocritical mockers in feasts
p: If we behave as a friend or brother, and mourn with them as their mother, and they still choose to persecute us? That's on them. They can rejoice in our pain, gather together to our hurt, but the Lord will see and repay.
p: They may make a feast of our lives, inviting other wicked men to join them, until they seemingly turn the whole world against us, yet we have the Lord.
big: Romans 8:31 | mark: If God be for us, who can be against us?

--- How long wilt thou look on?
kicker: Psalm 35 · Rescue my soul
big: Psalm 35:17 | mark: Lord, how long wilt thou look on?
p: Pleading with the Lord to not tarry, to come quickly to our aid, with the knowledge that we will sing His praises for His salvation, is a key piece of prayer.
p: We have discussed importunity in prayer, asking God again and again to please help us in our time of need. When we are past the point of what we think we can stand, to give us relief.
p: We have nothing to give but our love and praise in exchange.

--- Bargaining with God
kicker: Application • Prayer
p: Some try to bargain with God, usually when we pray amiss, seeking our will rather than His. "I'll go to church more, I will read my Bible more." I will stop this sin or that. We offer sacrifices of our own time or effort or even money.
p: But that's not what God seeks. Those are things that are the least we could do, even as unprofitable servants.
p: What we do have is praise for His power and His strength. And He is due all the glory.

--- In the great congregation
kicker: Psalm 35 · I will give thee thanks
big: Psalm 35:18
p: When we bargain with God for what we want, when we add our efforts and barter for His, we give ourselves a means to boast. We can fool ourselves into thinking our works played a part in the outcome. We begin to forget how helpless we were, and how God deserves all the glory. We begin to see our efforts as the reason God "owed it to me" rather than His love and righteousness.

--- All the glory
kicker: Application • Prayer
p: And so, we may keep asking God for His intervention, and we may remind Him that our gratitude will be expressed to His worship and to show others His strength and power.
p: But avoid bargaining or anything that shares the glory with Almighty God. Trust that He loves you and will, in His due time, make whatever it is you are going through to be handled to our good and His will.
big: Romans 8:28

--- Keep not silence
kicker: Psalm 35 · Mine enemies
scripture: Psalm 35:19-22 | mark: keep not silence: O Lord, be not far from me

--- Let them be confounded
kicker: Psalm 35 · Judge me, O Lord
scripture: Psalm 35:23-26 | mark: Judge me, O LORD my God, according to thy righteousness
p: Pray that our enemies be confounded. That those that do not fear the Lord come to see the bitter end of the wicked. Let man see His wrath on the unrighteous. Let the praise and rejoicing of His people reveal the mercy of God from Heaven.

--- Let the Lord be magnified
kicker: Psalm 35 · Shout for joy
scripture: Psalm 35:27-28 | mark: Let the LORD be magnified
center: Amen

